<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Walter Devereux, 1st Earl of Essex</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1541">1541</a>–<a href="/years/1576">1576</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Walter Devereux, 1st Earl of Essex, was an English nobleman who in 1573 led a private venture, backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a>, to colonise Clandeboye in Ulster. The enterprise was marked by brutality, including the seizure and execution of Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brian-macphelim-oneill">Brian MacPhelim O'Neill</a> in 1574 and the massacre on Rathlin Island in 1575, and it failed. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> as Earl Marshal of Ireland and was the father of Elizabeth's favourite, Robert Devereux.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/walter-devereux-1st-earl-of-essex.jpg" alt="Walter Devereux, 1st Earl of Essex" width="320" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Walter_Devereux,_1st_Earl_of_Essex_from_NPG.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>He was born at Carmarthen Castle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, the eldest son of Sir Richard Devereux, who died in 1547 in the lifetime of his own father, the 1st Viscount Hereford, and of Lady Dorothy Hastings, daughter of the 1st Earl of Huntingdon. Through his father's line he was related to the Bourchier family, earlier holders of the earldom of Essex. In 1561 or 1562 he married Lettice Knollys, daughter of Sir Francis Knollys and Catherine Carey; their children included Robert, Penelope, Dorothy and Sir Walter Devereux, who was killed at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a> in 1591.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On his grandfather's death on 27 September 1558 he became 2nd Viscount Hereford. He shared the custody of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-queen-of-scots">Mary, Queen of Scots</a>, in 1568, became Lord Lieutenant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/staffordshire">Staffordshire</a> in 1569 and served as high marshal of the field against the Northern Rebellion that year. He was created Earl of Essex on 4 May 1572 and made a Knight of the Garter on 17 June. He then offered to subdue or colonise part of Ulster at his own cost, and in August 1573 sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> with about 1,200 men.</p><p>A storm scattered his fleet, and wintering at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belfast">Belfast</a> his force shrank through sickness, famine and desertion to little more than 200 men. Quarrelling with the deputy Fitzwilliam and with the Queen, he turned to raids and massacres among the O'Neills. In October 1574 he seized Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brian-macphelim-oneill">Brian MacPhelim O'Neill</a> at a feast in Belfast Castle, killed his attendants and had him, his wife and his brother executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>. Ordered to break off the enterprise, he used the discretion left to him to defeat Sir Turlough Luineach O'Neill, and his men massacred several hundred of Sorley Boy MacDonnell's followers, chiefly women and children, hiding in the caves of Rathlin Island.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> at the end of 1575 but accepted the post of Earl Marshal of Ireland and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> in September 1576. Taken ill at a banquet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin-castle">Dublin Castle</a>, he died on 22 September 1576, probably of dysentery. Rumour said he had been poisoned at the behest of the 1st Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicester">Leicester</a>, who married his widow two years later, but a post-mortem found natural causes. His son Robert succeeded him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Walter Devereux, 1st Earl of Essex” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their territory was later contested by the MacDonnells and by English colonisation attempts, including the <b>Earl of Essex</b>'s massacre of Sir Brian MacPhelim O'Neill and his family in 1574.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-clandeboye">Lordship of Clandeboye</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Carmarthen Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.86,-4.31&amp;year=1563" aria-label="Carmarthen Castle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4453 371 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Walter Devereux, 1st Earl of Essex"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4903 641h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4903 641h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.86,-4.31&amp;year=1563">What was here in 1563? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
